markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Ethical Discussion Scenarios

#### Read each scenario below about a hypothetical ethical discussion. Think about how you might handle each one in the classroom by encouraging multiple perspectives, using "what-if" scenarios, or acknowledging uncertainty.

#### These scenarios don't have any *right* or *wrong* answers. Check out the thoughts for consideration section to further your own reflection.

<div style="background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 18px; border-left: 6px solid #2196f3; margin-bottom: 24px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6;">
<strong style="font-size: 22px;">Scenario 1: “Should We Use This AI Tool If It Consumes So Much Energy?”</strong>
<p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 18px;">
Your class is using AI Chatbot for the first time. <br><br>A student asks:
<em style="font-size: 18px;">“If using AI takes a lot of energy, should we avoid it? Are we hurting the environment just by doing this assignment?”</em>
<br><br>The question makes classmates uneasy and unsure how to respond.
</p>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 14px 16px; margin-top: 12px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 18px;">
<summary style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer;">🧐 Thoughts for Consideration</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 18px; margin-right: 6px; padding-bottom:10px; font-size: 18px;">
<h4 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">Acknowledge Uncertainty<br><br> Help students sit with the “gray areas”.</h4>
<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">
Use this moment to show that ethical questions about technology rarely have simple answers. You might say:
</p>

<p style="font-style: italic; font-size: 18px;">
“AI uses energy, and researchers are still studying how much different types of AI contribute to environmental impact. At the same time, AI can support learning, creativity, and problem-solving. So we make decisions by weighing benefits and costs, even when the information isn’t perfect.”
</p>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">Then help students think about responsible use by asking:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>When does using an AI tool add enough value to justify its energy use?</li>
<li>When might a simpler tool or approach work just as well?</li>
<li>How can we stay aware of environmental impact without feeling discouraged or powerless?</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">
Reinforce that uncertainty doesn’t stop us from acting responsibly—it encourages us to:
</p>

<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>Use technology with intention rather than habit.</li>
<li>Stay curious about how tools work and how they evolve.</li>
<li>Keep balancing environmental awareness with learning goals and real needs.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 18px; border-left: 6px solid #2196f3; margin-bottom: 24px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6;">
<strong style="font-size: 22px;">Scenario 2: “Is Facial Recognition Helpful or Harmful?” After-Video Discussion</strong>
<p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 18px;">
After watching a video about facial recognition—showing its use in unlocking phones, airport security, policing,
and retail—students react strongly.
</p>
<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">
Some say, <em>“This is scary and should be banned.”</em><br>
Others argue, <em>“It can catch criminals or find missing people, so we should use it.”</em><br><br>
The class splits into “all bad” versus “all good.”
</p>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 14px 16px; margin-top: 12px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 18px;">
<summary style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer;">🧐 Thoughts for Consideration</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 18px; margin-right: 6px; padding-bottom:10px; font-size: 18px;">
<h4 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">Encourage Multiple Perspectives<br><br>Move from “good vs. bad” to “who and when?”</h4>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">List the contexts shown in the video:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>Unlocking phones</li>
<li>Airport/border security</li>
<li>Law enforcement</li>
<li>Advertising and retail</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">Next, assign stakeholder roles:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li><strong>A teenager unlocking their phone</strong></li>
<li><strong>A store owner trying to prevent theft</strong></li>
<li><strong>A person misidentified due to system bias</strong></li>
<li><strong>A community concerned about constant surveillance</strong></li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">Discussion prompts:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>Who benefits most in each situation?</li>
<li>Who carries the biggest risks?</li>
<li>Are there contexts where most stakeholders would support it? Where they wouldn’t?</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">
Students discover that facial recognition can be <strong>both helpful and harmful</strong>,
depending on whose experience you focus on and the conditions of its use.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #e3f2fd; padding: 18px; border-left: 6px solid #2196f3; margin-bottom: 24px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.6;">
<strong style="font-size: 22px;">Scenario 3: Algorithmic Social Media Feeds</strong>
<p style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 18px;">
During a lesson on algorithms, students learn how social media platforms decide what to show them based on past
likes, clicks, comments, and watch time. You show how two users following the same accounts can still see very different feeds.
</p>
<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">
Some students say, <em>“This is great—I see content I like!”</em><br>
Others say, <em>“It manipulates people and hides important information.”</em>
</p>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 14px 16px; margin-top: 12px; border: 1px solid #ccc; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 18px;">
<summary style="font-size: 19px; font-weight: bold; cursor: pointer;">🧐 Thoughts for Consideration</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 18px; margin-right: 6px; padding-bottom:10px; font-size: 18px;">
<h4 style="margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px;">Use “What-If” Scenarios<br><br>Stretch thinking with thought experiments.</h4>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">Use imaginative “what-if” questions such as:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li><strong>What if</strong> the algorithm prioritized content that keeps people angry because that increases engagement?</li>
<li><strong>What if</strong> users could switch between different feed modes:
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>Chronological only</li>
<li>Diverse viewpoints</li>
<li>Friends and family first</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>What if</strong> platforms had to label why each post was shown (“Because you liked similar videos,” “Paid promotion,” etc.)?</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 8px; font-size: 18px;">Follow-up questions:</p>
<ul style="padding-left: 24px; font-size: 18px;">
<li>How might each change influence what people believe?</li>
<li>Who benefits or loses under each scenario?</li>
<li>Would the platform feel more trustworthy?</li>
</ul>

<p style="margin-top: 10px; font-size: 18px;">
What-if scenarios help students recognize that algorithmic feeds are <strong>design choices</strong>,
not neutral systems—and that alternative designs could lead to very different social outcomes.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>


MARKDOWN
